-- The baseline: the reference data the numbered migrations loaded.
--
-- The second half of the squash of every numbered migration through 00267,
-- kept apart from the schema so that nobody has to scroll seventy-five thousand
-- rows to reach the DDL.
--
-- These rows are a `pg_dump --data-only` of a database built by replaying those
-- migrations in order — NOT the old seed migrations concatenated. The
-- distinction is the whole point: later migrations deleted, re-pointed and
-- backfilled what earlier ones inserted. The Finnish and French trees were each
-- seeded from a national code list and then cut over to GeoNames, the postal
-- tables and the contract versions were each wiped and re-seeded, and the
-- location names were edited in place three times after seeding. Only the end
-- state is the answer, and only a dump of a replayed database has it.
--
-- WHAT IS HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT. Five tables: the location tree, the postal
-- codes, and the three lookup tables behind gedu contracts and account
-- consents. Every other table a from-migrations database holds is empty or
-- derived — `product_seat_counts` is trigger-maintained, and its rows on a live
-- database belong to that database's products.
--
-- WHY INSERT AND NOT COPY. The CLI splits a migration into statements and sends
-- them one at a time; a `COPY … FROM stdin` block desynchronises the wire
-- protocol and the connection is dropped mid-file. The rows are grouped a
-- thousand to a statement to keep the file's size close to the COPY form's.
-- They are positional, which is safe precisely here: the table they land in is
-- created by the schema file next to this one, and the two are written and
-- replaced together.
--
-- `locations` is ordered by depth. `locations.parent_id` points into the same
-- table, and a BEFORE INSERT trigger reads the parent's depth, so a child may
-- not arrive before its parent. `DEFAULT` in the tenth position is
-- `search_blob`, which the database generates from the three columns before it.
--
-- As with the schema file, production and staging never execute this: both had
-- already applied every migration it replaces, and the squash reached them by
-- editing the migration history table alone.



SET statement_timeout = 0;
SET lock_timeout = 0;
SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = 0;
SET transaction_timeout = 0;
SET client_encoding = 'UTF8';
SET standard_conforming_strings = on;
SELECT pg_catalog.set_config('search_path', '', false);
SET check_function_bodies = false;
SET xmloption = content;
SET client_min_messages = warning;
SET row_security = off;

